                DIGITAL AWARDED MOST COMPLETE SET OF
               XPG4 BRANDED COMPONENTS IN THE INDUSTRY


         DEC OSF/1 continues to lead in standards compliance


MAYNARD, Mass. -- June 6, 1994 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
announced today that it has received XPG4 branding in two important 
areas: base profile brand for the DEC OSF/1 operating system running 
on the Alpha AXP workstation and server families; XPG4 component 
brands for: 

     o 	 Internationalized System Calls and Libraries
     o	 Commands and Utilities
     o 	 C Language 
     o	 FORTRAN
     o 	 ADA
     o	 PASCAL
     o	 X Window System Display
     o	 X Window System Application Interface

     "Digital offers the most standards-compliant UNIX 
implementation in the industry," said John O'Keefe, vice president 
of UNIX Systems Marketing for Digital. "The award of the XPG4 brand 
for so many components of our UNIX offerings is testimony to the 
progress we have made to assure our customers of the inter-
operability, portability, and quality they have long expected from 
the UNIX operating system."

     The X/Open Common Applications Environment (CAE) is supported 
by an extensive set of conformance tests and a distinct X/Open 
trademark, the XPG brand, that is licensed by X/Open and may be 
carried only on products that comply with the X/Open CAE 
Specifications.

     The XPG brand, when associated with a vendor's product, 
communicates clearly and unambiguously to a procurer that the 
software bearing the brand correctly implements the corresponding 
X/Open CAE Specifications. Users specifying XPG-conformance in their 
procurement are therefore certain that the branded products they buy 
conform to the CAE Specifications. 

     Digital was the first vendor to be awarded branding for the 
XPG4 X Window System Display, and X Window System Application 
Interface on the DEC OSF/1 operating system V2.0.  These brands show 
that Digital's implementation of the X Window System is the most 
standard in the industry today. 

     Digital received the XPG4 base profile brand for Version 2.0 of 
the DEC OSF/1 operating system in earlier this year, along with the 
following component brands: XPG4 Internationalized System Calls & 
Libraries, Commands & Utilities, and C Language, Pascal, FORTRAN, 
Ada & Terminal Interfaces. 

     Finally, Digital was awarded XPG4 branding for DEC FORTRAN for 
DEC OSF/1 AXP V3.4. FORTRAN is the most popular programming language 
for high-end technical computing, and is widely employed in the 
research, scientific and engineering communities.

     "Just as we have met the standards expectations of our 
customers with DEC OSF/1 by providing over 99% of the programming 
interfaces included in the SPEC 1170 submitted to X/Open Ltd. last 
December, we will continue to comply with all relevant standards in 
the future," said O'Keefe.  "Version 2.0 of the DEC OSF/1 has been 
certified against FIPS151-2 by NIST, for example.  Customers will 
see us leading in UNIX standards across the board both with our own 
development projects and with technology which we obtain from 
others."

     The X Window System is a network-transparent windowing system, 
allowing graphically oriented programs to run on remote computers, 
yet have the display appear on any desktop in the network.  The X 
Window System allows graphical client/server communication between 
different vendors as well.  This powerful, vendor-neutral graphics 
paradigm has been adopted by the workstation industry and X/Open as 
the standard in network-transparent graphic systems.

     "The X Window System is the de facto windowing standard for 
UNIX systems," said O'Keefe.  "Digital was instrumental in both its 
development and bringing it forth as an industry standard.  We have 
been shipping it on our UNIX systems since 1986."

     X/Open is an independent, worldwide, open systems organization
supported by the world's largest information systems suppliers, user
organizations, and software companies.  Its mission is to bring to
users greater value from computing, through the practical 
implementation of open systems.

     The X Consortium is an independent, not-for-profit Delaware 
membership corporation with offices in Cambridge, Mass.  The purpose 
of the X Consortium is to foster the development, evolution, and 
maintenance of the X Window System, a comprehensive set of 
vendor-neutral, system-architecture neutral, network-transparent 
windowing and user interface standards.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems. Digital's scalable Alpha AXP 
platforms, storage, networking, software and services, together 
with industry-focused solutions from business partners, help 
organizations compete and win in today's global marketplace. 
