          DIGITAL ANNOUNCES CHANNELWORKS CUSTOMER WINS AND
                     INTERNATIONAL CERTIFICATION


  Customers Use Data Bridge to Access Cable Networks With Ethernet

                                   
NEW ORLEANS, LA -- May 23, 1994 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced several significant customer wins and international 
certification of its award-winning ChannelWorks product line, which 
was introduced at the National Cable Television Show a year ago. 

     The ChannelWorks product family is managed by the Emerging 
Network Technologies Division within the Network Product Business.  
The family of offerings convert cable TV systems into 2-way 
interactive, high-speed, metropolitan area networks that support 
video, voice and data applications throughout the community. 

     "In the past year, we've helped several cable television 
operators introduce to their subscribers the tremendous power of 
Ethernet-based services over the existing cable TV infrastructure.  
This lays the groundwork for real-life application of the 
much-discussed 'information superhighway,' " said Art Zins,
business segment manager of the Emerging Network Technologies 
Division. "Schools, hospitals and manufacturers are among our 
primary customers.  They're using these new networks to improve 
communication between sites.  And because they're able to eliminate 
point-to-point data links, they're dramatically reducing their 
telecommunications costs."

     Digital's ChannelWorks data bridge enables cable TV subscribers 
to receive and transmit digital video, audio and data over existing 
cable TV wiring.  The bridge permits this information to be carried 
at the standard 10 Mbps Ethernet, which has eight times the 
throughput of T-1 links that many companies currently use for data 
connections.

     Key business applications running on these networks include 
distance learning, in which instructors offer classes to students in 
remote locations via 1-way and 2-way video and discuss patient 
records, including X-rays and other digitized images; geographic
information systems; and multimedia kiosks.

     The ChannelWorks bridge, which is shipping in volume, allows 
subscribers worldwide to use this new technology to establish 
peer-to-peer networks with other subscribers in a common cable 
operator's metropolitan area network.  One bridge can link the
network of an entire building, and subscribers with wide- area fiber 
networks can often link entire campuses with a single bridge. The 
ChannelWorks bridge, which is linked directly to the subscriber's 
computer network and the incoming cable connection, is about the 
size of a standard cable TV converter, and each cable network can 
span a total distance of up to 70 miles. Sites improving their 
operations with ChannelWorks include:
The City of Worcester, Mass.

     Digital and Greater Media Cable helped this city link 25 public 
buildings, including the police and fire stations, town hall, the 
public library, the hospital and the school system.  The project 
allowed the city to eliminate T-1 connections and the associated 
monthly charges, thus reducing per connection costs substantially.

Arizona State University and Times-Mirror Cable  

     McDonnell Douglas Helicopter Systems and Allied Signal Engine 
Division are using Times-Mirror's cable network to communicate with 
suppliers in the Phoenix area, including Modern Industries, Tempe 
Precision Aircraft, Phoenix Heat Treating Inc., and Katchina Testing 
Laboratories.  This electronic commerce network  includes a diverse 
category of applications, such as: CAD, videoconferencing, shared 
whiteboards, electronic authorization and multimedia mail.  
Employees find communicating via the electronic commerce network 
replaces many face-to-face meetings and facilitates the entire 
product life cycle which ultimately accelerates time-to-market.
The trial, which will be evaluated by Arizona State University in 
late 1994, is intended to assess specific customer needs.  With over 
7,000 miles of coax and fiber in place, this project is one of the 
largest pilots examining the commercial use of cable networks.
Holland's Wageningen Agricultural University  

     The ChannelWorks bridge also has received international 
certification, and is deployed at Holland's Wageningen Agricultural 
University, where it links the 7,000-student university with the 
Agricultural Research Services agency and several private companies.  
Users can transmit as much as 400 pages of text per second across a 
22 kilometer fiber backbone.

     Plans for the project will evolve to include home-based access 
to the digital highway for students and private users.  Along with 
Digital Equipment B.V., a subsidiary of Digital Equipment Corp., and 
the university, co-sponsors of this project are NKM cable operating 
company and SURFnet, a Dutch national network for research and 
higher education.

     As a leader in networking, Digital will continue to work with 
the cable TV industry's multiple systems operators to develop new 
solutions for high-speed, broadband data communications over cable.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha AXP 
platforms, storage, networking, software and services, together 
with industry-focused solutions from business partners, help 
organizations compete and win in today's global marketplace.
