                DIGITAL ANNOUNCES SUPPORT FOR NOVELL
                        NetWare READY PROGRAM


       ...PATHWORKS integrates NetWare users with multivendor
                       enterprise networks...


MAYNARD, Mass. -- June 7, 1994 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced that its PATHWORKS network integration software 
supports Novell's NetWare Ready program, extending information 
access and resource sharing from the NetWare LAN to the enterprise-
wide network. 

     With PATHWORKS V5.0 for DOS and Windows software, organizations 
can dramatically increase the integration of NetWare users with 
multivendor PC NOS environments and enterprise networks through 
expanded management and client network services.  By incorporating 
NetWare technology, PATHWORKS makes it simple to configure PC
clients to access native Novell servers, in addition to applications 
and information on other systems in a multivendor distributed 
network. 

     "We are pleased that our joint development relationship with 
Novell has yielded PATHWORKS software that strengthens PC users' 
integration with enterprise networks," said Faye Allen, Digital's 
marketing manager for network software. 

     "Now customers who need both PC LANs and enterprise networks 
enjoy a competitive advantage by using Digital's PATHWORKS software 
to integrate Novell networks and Digital systems and networks," said 
Toby Corey, senior director of marketing for Novell's NetWare 
systems group. 

     PATHWORKS integrates the broadest selection of PCs, servers, 
network operating systems, network transports and protocols in the 
industry, without requiring costly custom integration. With 
PATHWORKS, PC users can not only use their NetWare IPX network 
transport, but can simultaneously load TCP/IP, DECnet, NetBEUI, and 
LAT transports. In addition, NetWare users can take advantage of
scalable OpenVMS systems as super-servers for multi-user business-
critical applications and client-server computing. 

     Included with PATHWORKS are the unique management capabilities 
of the ManageWORKS application -- the easiest way to administer a PC 
network with multivendor NOSs.  From a remote Microsoft Windows 
workstation, the administrator can manage NetWare 2.x and 3.x, LAN 
Manager, and PATHWORKS servers.  The consistent, powerful, PC-based 
graphical user interface of ManageWORKS can reduce administrative 
time and costs. 

     PATHWORKS is the most cost-effective solution for multi-server 
networks, in large part because the per seat license entitles a PC 
to access an unlimited number of servers.  Digital also offers 
concurrent-use licenses for customers who prefer to license their 
NOS by the server. Customers can mix both kinds of licenses. 

     PATHWORKS V5.0, available now, is priced at $205 per client for 
client network services as well as access to PATHWORKS file and 
print services, which is the license best suited for multi-server 
networks and enterprise solutions.  PATHWORKS (NetWare) server 
software is also available separately as a server-based license 
based on concurrent use, ranging in price from $3000 for 10 PCs to 
$18,750 for 250 users, which is best suited for workgroups with a 
single server. 

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha AXP 
platforms, storage, networking, software and services, together 
with industry-focused solutions from business partners, help
organizations compete and win in today's global marketplace. 
