            DIGITAL NAMES WILLIAM LOWE VICE PRESIDENT OF
                   WORLDWIDE STORAGE MANUFACTURING


            Lowe's 21 Years of Storage and Manufacturing
          Expertise Strengthen Digital's Global Operations

    
MAYNARD, Mass. -- June 7, 1994 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced it has appointed William Lowe, 47, to vice president 
of worldwide storage manufacturing.  Lowe, who will report to 
Charles F. Christ, vice president of Digital's Storage Business 
Unit, will be responsible for refining and expanding global 
manufacturing operations for the company's entire storage product 
line, including heads, magnetic and solid state disks, digital 
linear tape (DLT) drives, and StorageWorks subsystems and servers.  
Lowe was previously general manager of Digital's OEM Disk and Tape 
Storage Business, where he directed the storage products' 
engineering, sales, marketing and manufacturing efforts.

     "Bill Lowe has played a critical role in the tremendous growth 
of the OEM disk and tape business, and in helping establish our disk 
and DLT tape drives as industry-leading products," said Christ. 
"Bill's new appointment will help us aggressively focus on moving 
toward a stronger global manufacturing presence and further 
strengthen our leadership in the storage market."

     "This is an exciting time to be involved in shaping Digital's 
storage manufacturing efforts," said Lowe.  "By building on our 
strategy of establishing ourselves within hubs of storage activity, 
we will continue to expand significantly our worldwide manufacturing 
capabilities to meet customers' increasing demands."  

     Prior to his position as general manager of the OEM Disk and 
Tape Business, Lowe was a group manager of product materials and 
technology acquisition in the Storage Business Unit.  He was 
instrumental in negotiating Digital's joint venture with Storage 
Technology Inc., which resulted in the formation of Rocky Mountain 
Magnetics, established to develop magnetoresistive and thin-film 
heads design and production.  Lowe also served as disk manufacturing 
manager, heading the team that built Digital's first internally-
designed disk drive.  He holds a B.S. degree in business from 
Northeastern University and is a graduate of Harvard Business 
School's Program for Management Development.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha AXP 
platforms, storage, networking, software and services, together 
with industry-focused solutions from business partners, help 
organizations compete and win in today's global marketplace.
                                ####

