                NEW RESTART CENTRE PROVIDES MULTIVENDOR
          DISASTER RECOVERY FOR CUSTOMERS IN HONG KONG, CHINA


Hong Kong, China -- 23 June 1994 -- Digital Equipment Corporation
has opened a Digital Restart Centre at its Asia region headquarters in
Hong Kong to provide a multivendor disaster recovery service to
customers in the territory and South China.

     The Restart Centre will help customers recover fast from any event
that interrupts their computer operations, such as a flood or fire,
typhoon damage or electrical failure. The centre provides offsite data
storage and emergency computing resources so that customers can resume
operation with minimum disruption. Similar centres have recently been
opened in Singapore, Malaysia, Taiwan and Thailand.

     "Modern corporations are increasingly dependent upon computers and
high-speed data communications to conduct their businesses," said
Richard Tremblay, Operation Management Services Manager of Digital Asia.
"It is vital that they should protect their critical information systems
against possible disasters, however unlikely they may seem.

     "Digital's new Restart Centre is equipped with Digital's latest and
most powerful technology to provide back-up and recovery services for
customers in such industries as banking and finance, travel,
manufacturing, transportation and telecommunications. Companies that do
not have their own backup data centres can resume their business
critical operations at the Digital Restart Centre in a matter of hours."

     The Restart Centre forms part of Digital's comprehensive Business
Protection Services portfolio. Options include:

        o 100 per cent volume real-time shadowing of customer
          disks via FDDI wide-area networks.

        o Electronic vaulting, which creates a live image of the
          customer disk every four, six, eight or 12 hours
          through high-speed telecommunications lines.

        o Regular weekly back-up services over telephone lines.

        o Restoration and recovery of customer disks and tapes.

        o Offsite storage of customer back-up media.
 
     As part of the service, Digital commits to match or exceed Restart
Centre customers' critical computer resources. Equipment currently
installed at the new centre includes powerful VAX/VMS systems offering
more than 1,200 transactions per second and 60 gigabytes of data storage
and Alpha AXP systems running DEC OSF/1 and providing a further 25
gigabytes of disk storage. Additionally, the centre is equipped with
PATHWORKS and Novell NetWare servers, standalone PCs and a variety of
input/output devices.

     Digital consultants and networking specialists are available to
help business customers analyse the potential impact of a disaster, draw
up contingency plans and adopt backup strategies. Digital's restart
specialists can assist customers' IS staff to boot the operating system,
restore data and application software, monitor hardware performance,
back up daily files, monitor the configuration of the communications
network and interface with telecommunications vendors.

     Restart Centre customers are encouraged to perform regular practice
drills to ensure smooth operation of their contingency plans. In
addition, Digital has arrangements with other vendors to provide these
services to users of non-Digital systems.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated worldwide
information systems. Digital's scalable Alpha AXP platforms, storage,
networking, software and services, together with industry-focused
solutions from business partners, help organisations compete and win in
today's global marketplace. A US$13 billion company, Digital employs
more than 80,000 people in 100 countries. In Asia, it operates in 14
countries in addition to Japan and has manufacturing plants in India,
Indonesia, Malaysia, Singapore and Taiwan.
