                 DIGITAL ANNOUNCES NETWORK PRINTER
                  FOR NOVELL NETWARE ENVIRONMENTS


NEW YORK, NY -- June 28, 1994 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced a network printer that prints directly from Novell 
NetWare networks.  The PrintServer 17/600 Network Printer for 
NetWare environments, which has been Tested and Approved by Novell, 
includes, in the box, Digital's PrintServer software for NetWare 
systems, and network interfaces for Novell networks.  The printer 
enables PC users in NetWare environments to print directly from any 
application, with the bi-directional, real-time printer and print 
job status communication inherent in Digital's network printing 
software.  The announcement was made at the PC EXPO Conference, 
which opened here today at the Jacob Javits Convention Center.

     Digital's PrintServer 17/600 Printer for NetWare environments 
provides user selection of input and output tray, duplex 
(double-sided printing), or simplex (single-sided printing), 
automatic jam recovery, number-up formatting (multiple page images, 
up to 100, on a single sheet), automatic emulation detection and 
automatic conversion of ASCII to PostScript format -- all from 
remote locations. 

     "The extension of Digital's network printing leadership to 
Novell NetWare environments demonstrates our commitment to open 
systems," said Larry Cabrinety, Vice President of Digital's 
Components and Peripherals Business Unit.  "Desktop PC users on 
Novell NetWare networks can now join those on AT&T Unison, HP-UX, 
IBM AIX, Pyramid OSx, SCO UNIX, Sequent Dynix, Silicon Graphics I-
RIX, Solaris, SunOS, UNISYS System V, Windows NT, ULTRIX, DEC OSF/1 
AXP, OpenVMS networks, or any combination of these environments, to 
access the level of network printing which only Digital can 
provide." 

     Digital's 70,000 impression/month PrintServer 17/600 Printer 
for NetWare environments includes Thin and Twisted Pair Ethernet 
connectors, supports NetWare V3.11, V3.21 and V4.x, and uses 
NetWare's built-in TCP/IP stack and Novell's recommended bindery 
emulation (for V4.x users).  The printer includes Adobe PostScript 
Level 2, PCL-5, 600 X 600 dpi resolution, is priced at $4,695, and 
is available in August, 1994.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha AXP 
platforms, storage, networking, software and services, 
together with industry-focused solutions from business partners, 
help organizations compete and win in today's global marketplace. 
