                    DIGITAL ANNOUNCES INTENTION
        TO PHASE OUT OPERATIONS AT WESTFIELD, MASSACHUSETTS
                 ENCLOSURES MANUFACTURING FACILITY


MAYNARD, Mass. -- June 30, 1994 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
informed its employees today that it intends to phase out the metal 
enclosures manufacturing operation at its Westfield, Massachusetts 
facility. The plant, which currently employs approximately 340 
people, manufactures the metal housings for computers. 

     The process for the phase-out will be a gradual reduction of 
the workforce over the course of the next nine months. The 
enclosures work done at the facility will be outsourced to existing 
businesses outside the company. It is expected that the process 
will be complete by March of 1995.

     The details of the process were outlined today to employees in 
a group meeting. Digital will offer the affected employees a number 
of benefits including a financial support package based on years of 
service to the corporation; continuation of medical, dental and 
life insurance for a specified period; and professional 
outplacement assistance.

     Dan Jennings, Digital's vice president of Worldwide 
Manufacturing said, "Digital has embarked on the next phase of our 
strategic redirection program designed to strengthen and sharpen 
our focus, and enhance our competitive position in core markets to 
resume profitable growth.  Our manufacturing strategy centers 
around investing in businesses that support our core competencies 
and divesting ourselves of those that do not differentiate us.  
While we are very proud of the contribution the Metal Enclosures 
Manufacturing Unit has made to our product offerings, it does not 
represent a core competency for the company. The difficult decision 
to close our Westfield plant is one facet of our ongoing commitment 
to a return to profitability." 

     The disposition of the site, which is owned by Digital, has 
not yet been determined. The corporation will be working with state 
and local governments, as well as outside organizations, to find an 
external alternate use for the plant.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems. Digital's scalable Alpha AXP 
platforms, storage, networking, software and services, together 
with industry-focused solutions from business partners, help 
